he e6-pawn, but the race toward the kings quickly took over the board."},{"text":"Your advance with 11...a3 cracked open White's shelter on the b-file. After White pushed 13.g5, your retreat 13...Ne8 unleashed a discovered attack down the f-file against the knight on f3. White tried to reposition with 15.Na4, but this left the knight directly in the path of 15...b5.","line":"11...a3 12.b3 c6 13.g5 Ne8"},{"text":"White ignored the threat on the flank and pushed 16.h5, allowing you to win a piece with 16...bxa4. You then activated your bishop with 18...Bb4 and 19...Bc3 to control key squares near White's king. After 21...Qb4 created decisive threats on the open b-file, White resigned.","line":"16...bxa4 17.bxa4 d5 18.exd5 Bb4 19.g6 Bc3 20.gxh7+ Kxh7 21.Nh4 Qb4"}],"turning_point":"White's blunder 16.h5 allowed you to play 16...bxa4, winning a piece and breaking through the defense around the white king.","focus":"Keep an eye on tactical vulnerabilities in your camp during the opening, such as the loose e6-pawn after 8...Nbd7.","model":"google/gemini-3.7-flash","v":2,"cost":0.007863,"at":1793768577778},"style":{"top":[["Wilhelm Steinitz",0.247],["Boris Spassky",0.212],["Vasily Smyslov",0.081]],"why":["quiet rooks","opposite-side castling","pressure on the enemy king"]},"style_v":1}
